id: pm-odohgb
title: "Workspace federation: cross-workspace references, remote dependency resolution, and aggregated bounded context without a central server"
description: "The federation story is filed as an organizational need with no implementation owner. It is the natural scale boundary: a single workspace scales by index and packing work already underway, but an organization with many repositories has many workspaces, and the questions that matter cross them — what blocks this item lives in another repository's tracker, what is the aggregate state of a programme spanning eight repositories, which agent in which workspace holds the claim on the shared dependency. Design this the way the rest of pm is designed: no central server, no live coupling, git-native. A workspace declares peers by repository coordinate; cross-workspace references are first-class identifiers that resolve through a locally cached, verifiable snapshot of the peer's public projection; aggregation is a read-side fold over those snapshots with explicit staleness reporting, never a distributed transaction. Writes stay strictly local — a federated reference can be observed and depended upon but never mutated remotely — which keeps the immutability and merge guarantees intact and makes the whole feature safe to adopt incrementally. Cross-workspace cycles, unreachable peers, and stale snapshots must be reported as facts in the output rather than treated as errors, because in a federation partial knowledge is the normal state."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 3
tags[6]: "area:sdk","area:storage",federation,long-horizon,multi-agent,scale
created_at: "2026-07-25T12:30:34.401Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T13:58:38.483Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 1200
acceptance_criteria: "A workspace declares peers by repository coordinate, and cross-workspace references are first-class identifiers distinguishable from local ones in every projection; Peer state is a locally cached, integrity-verified snapshot of the peer's public projection, and every read reports the snapshot's age; Aggregation across peers is a bounded read-side fold that honors the token budget and reports partial results with named missing peers rather than failing; Writes to a peer workspace are impossible through federation, asserted by a test that attempts every mutation path against a federated reference; A cross-workspace dependency cycle is detected and reported as a fact with the participating workspaces named, never as a crash or a silent drop; An unreachable peer degrades to last-known state with explicit staleness, and a workspace with no peers behaves exactly as it does today, proven by snapshot; Federated identifiers survive merge, history replay, and format migration with the same guarantees as local identifiers"
goal: project management = context management
objective: Context assembly answers questions that span repositories without any workspace giving up local authority
value: The largest deployments are inherently multi-repository; without federation the product's ceiling is one repository regardless of how well a single workspace scales
impact: Introduces the first identifiers that point outside a workspace; the design must keep write authority local or the immutability and merge guarantees weaken
outcome: "An organization runs one tracker per repository and still gets programme-level context, dependency resolution, and aggregation"
parent: pm-o2kc
risk: high
confidence: 50
