comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-15T13:58:37.867Z","harness:codex","Federation design without a mandatory central server: use globally scoped workspace identities plus local item IDs, signed workspace manifests, explicit remote relationship types, versioned schema/capability negotiation, and trust policies that distinguish authoritative, cached, unavailable, stale, and unverified data. Resolve context through bounded parallel fan-out, per-remote budgets/deadlines, local cache and freshness receipts, top-k score calibration, deterministic partial-result merging, and continuation tokens that preserve each remote cursor. Support offline-first operation and partial replication by exchanging content-addressed event segments, Merkle summaries, causal watermarks/vector summaries, and compensating events; never rewrite a remote history or collapse an unresolved cross-workspace conflict. Security boundaries include allowlisted fields, credential isolation, provenance/attestation verification, hostile payload limits, recursion/cycle budgets, and tenant-safe redaction. Validate on one-item ephemeral projects, multi-decade archives, million-item shards, network partitions, schema skew, repository moves, remote deletion, resurrection, and thousand-agent fan-in. Publish completeness, staleness, trust, omission, latency, token, and unresolved-reference receipts so an agent can distinguish a complete answer from the best available partial answer."
body: ""
